---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import TrainingLayout from '../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro';

const labs = (await getCollection('labs')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<TrainingLayout title="Labs" currentPath="/labs">
  <h1>Hands-on Labs</h1>
  <p>
    Each domain has a lab with one task per training section. The labs use <strong>Acme Support</strong>, a small
    multi-tenant support assistant with retrieval, tools, an emergency brake, budgets, and a hash-chained audit log.
    Its defaults are deliberately naive. You turn on and build the controls, then measure attack success and false
    refusals with the lab's scoring tool. All data is fake and nothing leaves your machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is pure Python 3.11+ with no dependencies. It runs offline with a deterministic stand-in model, or against any
    OpenAI-compatible endpoint for real measurements. Get it from the
    <a href="https://github.com/mrcloudchase/caige/tree/main/lab">lab directory of the cAIge repository</a>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/mrcloudchase/caige.git
cd caige/lab
python3 -m unittest
python3 -m caige_lab eval --suite suites/benign.jsonl</code></pre>

  <div class="section-tiles">
    {labs.map((l) => (
      <a href={`/labs/${l.data.slug}`} class="section-tile">
        <span class="section-tile-number">Lab {l.data.order} · {l.data.estimatedTime}</span>
        <span class="section-tile-title">{l.data.title}</span>
        <span class="section-tile-desc">{l.data.description}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
</TrainingLayout>
